`,s.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The problem isn't that information is missing anymore. The problem is that ",s.jsx(t.strong,{children:"different people now believe different histories."})]}),`
`,s.jsxs(t.p,{children:["This behavior, presenting different, conflicting truths to different people at the same time, is called ",s.jsx(t.strong,{children:"equivocation"}),"."]}),`
`,s.jsx(t.p,{children:'Suddenly, our simple majority rule breaks down completely. Bob writes down "Soup" and confirms it with his majority. Charlie writes down "Steak" and confirms it with his majority. Both groups think they have a valid, committed order history, but the kitchen is about to cook two completely different meals for the same table.'}),`
`,s.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The moment a participant can lie or pass around corrupted state, ",s.jsx(t.strong,{children:"counting simple majorities is no longer enough"}),". The system can no longer just ask ",s.jsx(t.em,{children:'"How do we preserve our history?"'})," It first has to ask ",s.jsx(t.strong,{children:'"How do we prove we are even looking at the same history?"'})]}),`
`,s.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Part 3: Rebuilding Trust from Scratch"}),`
`,s.jsxs(t.p,{children:["This broader, zero-trust failure environment is called the ",s.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Byzantine failure model"}),". It assumes that nodes can crash, experience lag, suffer bit-rot in memory, or actively transmit contradictory data to their peers."]}),`
`,s.jsx(t.p,{children:"To survive in a world where nodes can equivocate, the protocol has to fundamentally alter how machines talk to one another:"}),`
